Third-party and earlier code in this repository

Physlib, https://github.com/leanprover-community/physlib, commit 08a6c1b5b8e6db340547142c3f7047635128c786,
Apache-2.0. The full licence text is the file LICENSE of this repository (the Apache-2.0 text, byte-identical to
Physlib's LICENSE at that commit); Physlib has no NOTICE file. Copyright holder as evidenced by the headers of the
upstream files: Tom Ole Diem ("Copyright (c) 2026 Tom Ole Diem. All rights reserved.", "Authors: Tom Ole Diem").
The upstream copyright notice and a statement of the changes are kept in the header of each adapted file.

- RealJordanFundamental.lean adapts 13 declarations of
  PhyslibAlpha/ProbabilisticTheory/JordanOrderUnit/Quadratic/Fundamental.lean (11 proof bodies token-identical,
  two endpoints renamed; upstream git blob 37fa7e0e at the commit above).
- JordanPowerAssociativity.lean adapts all eight declarations of
  PhyslibAlpha/ProbabilisticTheory/JordanOrderUnit/Power/Associative.lean; the proof bodies are identical after
  renaming jpow_* to elementPower_*, Module.End.mul_apply to mul_apply_eq_comp and E to P.Element (upstream git
  blob 2b455936 at the commit above). No upstream module is imported.
- RealJordanOperators.lean adapts the definitions mulLeft, quadRep, innerDerivation, jordanTriple and the lemmas
  mulLeft_apply, quadRep_apply, innerDerivation_apply, innerDerivation_swap, jordanTriple_outer_comm,
  jordanTriple_diag of PhyslibAlpha/ProbabilisticTheory/JordanOrderUnit/Operator.lean and Quadratic/Triple.lean
  (definitions identical up to names and notation; innerDerivation_swap and jordanTriple_outer_comm with
  token-identical proofs). The comparison was made against an October 2026 Physlib snapshot with the same author
  header, not against the files at the commit above.
- UnitalJordanProduct.lean, JordanProductQuadratic.lean and EuclideanJordanForm.lean follow Physlib's conventions;
  a declaration-body scan of every development file against the Physlib sources found matching bodies only in the
  three files above.

Thanks to Tom Ole Diem and the Physlib contributors.

